    "lyrics": {
      "text": "What de-light of life re-main-eth no par-ty to grief? What glor-y stand--eth im-mu-ta-ble on earth? All are more fee-ble than shad-ows; all more de-lud-ing than dreams. Yet one mo-ment on-ly and death__ shall sup-plant them all. But in the light of Thy coun-ten-ance, O Christ, and in the sweet-ness of Thy beau---ty, grant rest un-to those whom Thou hast cho-sen, since Thou art the Friend__ of__ man.\n\n\n\nEve-ry man__ fad-eth like a flow-er, and like a dream__ doth he pass a-way and is dis-solved; and a-gain, when the trum-pet shall sound, all the dead__ shall a-rise, as though in an earth-quake, to meet Thee, O Christ God. Then, O Mas-ter, es-tab-lish in the tab-er-nac-les of Thy Saints the spir-its of Thy ser--vants whom Thou hast trans-lat-ed from a-mong us, O Christ. \n\nWoe is__ me! What man-ner of or-deal__ doth the soul en-dure when it is part-ed from the bod-y! Woe is__ me! How man--y then are its tears; and there is none to show mer-cy! It turn--eth its eyes__ to the An-gels; all un-a-vail-ing is its prayer. It stretch-eth out its hands to men, and__ find-eth none to suc-cour. Where-fore, my be-lov-ed breth--ren, med-i-tat-ing on the brev-i-ty of our life, let__ us be-seech of Christ rest for those that have de-par-ted hence, and for our souls great mer-cy.\n\n\n\nAll mor-tal things__ that ex-ist not af-ter death are van-i--ty. Rich-es a-bide not, nei-ther doth glo-ry ac-com-pan-y us on the__ way; for when death__ com-eth, all these things van-ish ut-ter--ly. Where-fore, let us cry__ un-to Christ, the Im-mor-tal__ King: Un-to them that have been trans-lat-ed from a-mong__ us, do Thou grant rest, in the a-bode of all them that re-joice__ in__ Thee.\n\n\n\nTru-ly dread-ful is the mys-ter-y of death! For how is the soul sep-a-rat--ed with vi-o-lence from bod-y and joints and all, that nat-u-ral un-ion; and by the di-vine__ will, that bond that is most nat-u-ral is cut a-sun--der and whol-ly rent a-part? We there-fore warm-ly pray of Thee: Do Thou grant rest to those tak-en hence in the tents of Thy just on high, O Life-giv--er and Friend of man.\n\nWhere is at-tach-ment to the world? Where is the il-lu-sion of things fleet--ing? Where is the gold and sil--ver? Where__ is the mul-ti-tude of house-hold ser-vants and their clam--our? All is dust,__ all is ash-es, all is shad-ows. But come,__ let us cry__ out to the Im-mor-tal King: O Lord, count wor-thy of Thine e-ter-nal good things them that have been trans-lat-ed from a-mong__ us, and grant them rest_- in Thine age-less bless-ed-ness.\n\n\n\nI_ called to mind the Proph-et who cried: I am earth and ash--es; and I looked a-gain in-to the graves, and be-held the bones laid bare, and I said: Who, then, is the king or war-ri-or, the rich man or the pau-per, the right--eous man or the sin--ner? Yet, with the right--eous grant rest un-to Thy ser--vants, O Lord, since Thou art the Friend of__ man.\n\n\n\nThy cre-a-tive com-mand be-came mine or-i-gin and__ foun-da-tion; for wish-ing to fash-ion me as a liv-ing crea--ture out of na-ture vis---i-ble and in-vis-i-ble, Thou didst fash--ion my bod-y from the earth and didst give me a soul by Thy di-vine and quick-’ning breath. Where---fore, O Sav--iour, grant rest un-to Thy ser-vants in the land of__ the liv-ing, in the tab-er-nac-les of the right-eous.\n\n\n\nGrant rest,__ O our life--giv-ing Sav--iour, to our breth-ren whom Thou hast trans-lat-ed from things tem-por-al, as they cry: O Lord, glo-ry be__ to__ Thee. \n\nWhen in the be-gin--ning Thou hadst cre-at-ed man ac-cor-ding to Thine im--age and like--ness, Thou didst set him in Par-a-dise to reign ov--er Thy crea--tures. But when he was be-guiled by the en-vy of the dev--il and par-took__ of the food, he be-came__ a trans-gres--sor of Thy com-mand--ments. For which cause, O Lord, Thou didst con-demn him to re-turn a-gain un-to the earth whence he was tak-en, and to ask__ for re-pose.\n\n\n\nI_ weep and I la-ment when I think up-on death, and be-hold our beau---ty, which was fash-ioned af--ter the im-age of God, ly-ing in the tomb dis-fig-ured, dis-hon-oured, be-reft of form. O won--der! What__ is this my-ster-y that hath be-fall-en us? How have we been giv-en o-ver un-to cor-rup-tion? How have we been yoked to-geth-er with death? Of a truth, as it is writ--ten, it is by__ the com-mand of__ God, Who grant-eth rest to the de-part-ed.\n\nGlor-y to the Fa-ther, and to the Son, and to the Ho-ly Spir-it.\n\nThy death, O Lord,__ is be-come the cause of im-mor-tal-i-ty. For if Thou hadst not been laid__ in a grave, Par-a-dise would not have been o-pened. Where--fore, do Thou grant rest__ un-to them that have been trans-lat-ed hence, since Thou art the Friend__ of__ man.\n\nBoth now and ev-er and un-to the a--ges of a-ges. A-men.\n\nO_ pure Vir--gin, the gate of the Word and Moth-er of our God: Pray thou that their souls__ be shown mer--cy.____"
    }
  }
}